Template-Type: ReDIF-Article 1.0
Author-Name: Sutedjo Sutedjo
Title: Accounting Earnings, Current Tax Expense, and Stock Returns: Evidence from Indonesia’s LQ45 Firms
Abstract: This study evaluates contemporaneous associations of reported basic earnings and current tax expense with annual stock returns in a retrospective panel of Indonesia’s LQ45 firms. The May–July 2026 constituent list is backfilled over 2017–2024. After a pre-specified tax-regime exclusion and removal of ten pre-listing or IPO-year observations without full-calendar-year returns, the sample contains 299 firm-years from 43 firms. Returns are split- and dividend-adjusted; continuous variables are winsorized at P1–P99. Firm fixed effects with firm-clustered inference are supplemented by two-way clustering, year effects, an exact wild year-cluster bootstrap, untreated variables, and a next-year-return test. Earnings scaled by split-adjusted beginning price are positively associated with contemporaneous total shareholder return (coefficient = 1.698; p = 0.001; 95% CI [0.728, 2.668]). An interquartile-range change corresponds to a 14.7-percentage-point return difference. Current tax expense scaled by beginning market capitalization is insignificant (coefficient = 0.545; p = 0.506). Neither measure predicts next-year return. The current-constituent sampling frame, eight-year horizon, attribution mismatch in consolidated tax expense, and non-event timing limit causal and predictive inference. The revision contributes a transparent corporate-action audit, reproducible sample flow, direct EPS-to-price measurement, and few-cluster inference for an Indonesian LQ45 setting.
Keywords: Accounting Earnings,current tax expense,Indonesia,LQ45,panel data,Stock Returns
Journal: International Journal of Management, Accounting and Economics
Pages: 1413-1431
Volume: 13
Issue: 11
Year: 2026
DOI: 10.22034/ijmae.2026.586344.2033
