Template-Type: ReDIF-Article 1.0
Author-Name: Augustine Kajembe
Author-Name: Lucas Mataba
Author-Name: Goodluck Mmari
Author-Name: William Warsanga
Title: Determinants of Technical Efficiency in Private Solid Waste Management Firms in Dar es Salaam, Tanzania
Abstract: The global drive for sustainable urban services is reshaping solid waste management systems, and technical efficiency has emerged as a key challenge for many firms, especially in developing nations. This study examines firm-specific and external operating conditions influencing technical efficiency among private solid waste management firms in Dar es Salaam, Tanzania. The study employed unbalanced panel data from a census of 36 private firms from 2016 to 2023. Technical efficiency scores were estimated using Stochastic Frontier Analysis, yielding a mean score of 52.5%, indicating technical inefficiency among private firms in Dar es Salaam. The determinants of technical efficiency were analysed using a Two‑Step System Generalised Method of Moments Model. The results show that firm age, firm size, container method of waste collection, new technology adopted, and the planned nature of the area were positively associated with technical efficiency, while waste collection frequency, contract duration, training, and distance to the dumpsite were negatively associated with it. Customers served, mixed technology adoption, mixed waste-collection methods, and mixed-nature areas did not significantly influence technical efficiency. These findings highlight the importance of strengthening firm-specific capabilities and managing external operating factors to enhance technical efficiency in private SWM firms. Therefore, municipal authorities should improve access to unplanned service areas and consider greater use of container collection methods, while firm managers should invest in appropriate new technology and optimise waste collection frequency.
Keywords: solid waste management,System GMM,Technical Efficiency,Time-Varying Stochastic Frontier Analysis
Journal: International Journal of Management, Accounting and Economics
Pages: 1432-1463
Volume: 13
Issue: 11
Year: 2026
