Template-Type: ReDIF-Article 1.0
Author-Name: Yamini Mohan
Author-Name: P. Na. Kanchana
Title: Consumer Attitudes and Purchase Intention toward Organic Food Products in Tamil Nadu: Evidence from Tiruchirappalli District
Abstract: The behavioural determinants of organic food purchase intention must be identified to enable evidence-based marketing strategies and policy interventions that promote sustainable food consumption. While individual predictors of organic food purchase have been examined extensively, the mediating role of consumer attitude and the joint effect of psychological, social, and economic determinants have rarely been tested in Indian tier-two cities. This study develops and empirically tests an extended Theory of Planned Behaviour (TPB) model that positions consumer attitude as the mediator linking health consciousness, environmental concern, brand image, social influence, and price sensitivity to organic food purchase intention in Tiruchirappalli District, Tamil Nadu. A cross-sectional survey of 350 adult organic-food consumers was conducted at ten retail sites using a structured bilingual (Tamil/English) five-point Likert questionnaire. The empirical analysis combines exploratory factor analysis with a two-equation OLS path model and Baron-Kenny mediation tests, supplemented by Sobel statistics. Health consciousness (β = 0.475, p < .001) and environmental concern (β = 0.367, p < .001) jointly explained 57.2% of the variance in consumer attitude. The second equation explained 61.5% of the variance in purchase intention, with health consciousness (β = 0.353, p < .001), consumer attitude (β = 0.314, p < .001), brand image (β = 0.191, p < .001), and social influence (β = 0.084, p = .029) as positive correlates and price sensitivity (β = -0.104, p = .003) as a negative correlate. The significant indirect effect is consistent with partial mediation, with Consumer Attitude accounting for approximately 29.6% of the association between Health Consciousness and Purchase Intention (indirect effect = 0.149, Sobel z = 5.16, p < .001), accounting for approximately 29.6% of the total effect. The study provides empirical evidence consistent with a partial attitudinal-mediation mechanism within an extended TPB framework applied to an emerging-market, tier-two-city context. Practical implications for organic-food retailers, certification bodies, and policymakers are discussed, alongside explicit limits to causal inference.
Keywords: Consumer attitudes,Emerging markets,Environmental concern,Health Consciousness,organic food,Purchase Intention
Journal: International Journal of Management, Accounting and Economics
Pages: 1464-1484
