Template-Type: ReDIF-Article 1.0
Author-Name: Savanam Chandra Sekhar
Title: Artificial Intelligence, Automation, and Labor Demand: An Economic Modeling Approach
Abstract: Artificial intelligence (AI) is transforming production systems, labor markets, and organizational decision-making by enhancing productivity while reshaping labor demand and workforce skill requirements. However, existing research has largely examined AI adoption from technological or managerial perspectives, with limited integration of economic theory and firm-level evidence. Addressing this gap, this study develops and empirically validates an economic modeling framework grounded in production economics, labor demand theory, skill-biased technological change, and task-based automation theory to explain the effects of AI-driven automation on organizational labor demand and productivity. A cross-sectional explanatory research design was employed using survey data from 412 managers across manufacturing, information technology, logistics, banking, retail, healthcare, and telecommunications sectors. The proposed framework integrates the Cobb–Douglas production function with labor demand equations and is validated using reliability and validity analysis, multiple regression, analysis of variance, and structural equation modeling. The findings reveal that AI-driven automation significantly reduces demand for routine labor while improving organizational productivity and operational efficiency. Higher labor costs encourage automation investment, whereas workforce skill intensity and technological adaptability strengthen human–AI complementarity and mitigate labor displacement. Significant industry differences in automation intensity are also observed, with manufacturing and information technology exhibiting higher adoption levels than service-oriented sectors. By integrating economic production theory with empirical organizational evidence, the study advances understanding of AI-enabled labor market dynamics and provides practical implications for managers and policymakers seeking to promote workforce reskilling, strategic AI adoption, and sustainable technological transformation.
Keywords: Automation investment,labor demand,Organizational Productivity,skill intensity,workforce reskilling
Journal: International Journal of Management, Accounting and Economics
Pages: 1485-1524
Volume: 13
Issue: 11
Year: 2026
