Template-Type: ReDIF-Article 1.0
Author-Name: Dorcas Gonese
Author-Name: Regina Stofile
Title: <span lang="EN-ZA">Innovation and Self-Employment in South Africa: The Moderating Effect of Institutional Quality</span>
Abstract: South Africa has been acknowledged as one of the top three global innovation leaders in sub-Saharan Africa. It ranks 69th globally and is second in the region, behind Mauritius (55th). Despite this status, the country is characterised by a sustained challenge of elevated unemployment, particularly among its youth. This trend highlights a contradictory trend that requires thorough econometric and empirical assessment to inform policymakers and governments on effective strategies for self-employment and overall job creation. Therefore, this study seeks to assess the impact of national innovation capacity on self-employment in South Africa, while accounting for the moderating effects of institutional quality. The study utilises the Autoregressive Distributed Lag (ARDL) estimation technique with the annual data spanning from 2006 to 2025. The study's results indicate that innovation has a direct positive impact on self-employment in both the short- and long-run in South Africa. Furthermore, institutional quality has a positive direct effect on self-employment. However, regarding the interaction effects, institutional quality reduces the positive effect of innovation on self-employment in South Africa. The study recommends that the South African government and policymakers adopt an integrated policy framework that synchronises innovation with institutional reform to transform potential economic displacement into sustainable self-employment growth.
Keywords: ARDL,Innovation Capacity,institutions,Self-Employment,South Africa
Journal: International Journal of Management, Accounting and Economics
Pages: 1525-1558
