Template-Type: ReDIF-Article 1.0
Author-Name: Mohsen Mohammadi Khyareh
Title: From Domestic Credit to New Business Density Through Financial Inclusion: A Mediation Analysis
Abstract: In market-oriented economies, domestic credit to the private sector has been shown to correlate strongly with new business formation. The present study addresses the causal pathway through which credit influences new firm registrations remains unidentified by asking whether financial inclusion mediates the relationship between domestic credit and new business density. The paper employs a panel mediation framework on a balanced sample of 95 high- and middle-income countries observed at (2007–2024). A financial inclusion index is constructed via principal component analysis of bank account ownership, digital payment usage, and the share of credit intermediated by deposit money banks. The three-step mediation procedure adapted from Baron and Kenny (1986) is implemented with country fixed effects, and the indirect effect is tested using bootstrapped confidence intervals in a panel corrected standard errors specification. Domestic credit is a strong positive predictor of financial inclusion. Financial inclusion, in turn, robustly increases new business density. When the mediator is added, the direct effect of domestic credit on new business registrations shrinks by approximately 37 per cent and is only marginally significant, while the indirect effect through financial inclusion remains highly significant. These results confirm that financial inclusion serves as a central transmission channel. Regulatory quality, economic growth, and urbanization continue to exhibit positive and statistically significant correlations with new business density. This paper makes a distinct contribution by transforming the baseline study’s reduced form finding into a mechanism-based explanation. It demonstrates that simply expanding the volume of domestic credit is insufficient; credit must be paired with an inclusive financial infrastructure that enables potential entrepreneurs to access it. By isolating the indirect path, the study provides clear guidance for policy: financial inclusion infrastructure is not a complement but a prerequisite for credit driven entrepreneurship.
Keywords: Domestic credit,Entrepreneurship,Financial Inclusion,Mediation,new business density,panel data
Journal: International Journal of Management, Accounting and Economics
Pages: 1559-1584
Volume: 13
Issue: 11
Year: 2026
